#!/usr/bin/env bash
# PreToolUse hook — the floor of the spec-driven workflow (specs/spec-driven-workflow, ADR-003;
# approval hardened in specs/hook-owned-approval, ADR-001; the cycle's limits added in
# specs/factory-cycle).
#
# Praxis primes; it did not gate. The measured evaluation recorded what priming alone buys
# for the record of a change: 0 Run Cards in 8/8 `full` cells. A prose rule saying "write the
# spec first" travels the same channel and gets the same result. So the one invariant the
# owner asked for — every change leaves `specs/<change>/`, in every mode — is held here,
# mechanically, and scaled by the dial:
#
#   fast        `specs/<active>/implementation.md` exists
#   full, deep  `specs/<active>/spec.md` (a legacy `design.md` is accepted) and
#               `implementation.md` exist, AND the person approved the spec:
#               `.praxis/approved/<active>` exists → allow, whatever `stage:` says. No marker →
#               the ONE approval stop, as the host's own primitive, `permissionDecision: "ask"`.
#               When the person approves, the edit runs and `post-tool-use` writes the marker;
#               the floor never asks again. No spec → deny, naming the file to write.
#
# Why a marker and not `stage:`: evals/floor measured 3/3 unattended `full` runs setting
# `stage: planned` themselves after the host refused the `ask`, then building. The ledger is
# a field the model owns; the marker is written by a hook after an edit the person allowed,
# or by the person through `/praxis:approve <change>`. Edit/Write targeting
# `.praxis/approved/` or `.praxis/needs-human/` is denied here so the model cannot forge
# either record (Bash writes remain a documented gap). In `claude -p` the host refuses `ask` —
# that is the intended outcome: an unattended run must ship the marker, written by a person
# or by CI.
#
# Only source edits are gated. Writes under `specs/`, `.praxis/` (except the two marker
# directories), `agents/` and to the root instruction files are always allowed: that is how
# the agent creates the record the floor asks for. Targets outside the project root are not
# this project's business. One spec-side exception: the main thread may write under
# `specs/<c>/review/round-*/` only a file whose verdict is SKIP — the orchestrator's record of
# a lens that never returned; every other review file is its lens's to write.
#
# A deny must be followable by the model that receives it. It cannot read the plugin
# directory (in `claude -p` that is a refusal; interactively, a permission prompt), so on the
# first deny the templates are copied into the project as `specs/_templates/` and every step
# refers to that local copy: Read it, then Write `specs/<name>/<file>`. The required sections
# are repeated in the message so a model that still cannot read produces the right shape.
#
# `<active>` is `.praxis/active-change` when present, else the most recently modified
# ledger.
#
# Three more gates ride on tools that carry no file path (specs/factory-cycle, design.md
# appendix). Each reads only files under `specs/<change>/`, so nothing depends on the
# conversation:
#
#   Agent        a dispatch whose subagent_type is `builder` (`praxis:builder` too)
#   SendMessage  a resume whose `to` is the builder id the ledger records
#                → round 0: allow · round N with a lens file missing: deny, naming the
#                  missing lenses (the barrier) · round N complete, all PASS: allow · round 1
#                  complete with CHANGES: allow (the one fix round) · round 2 complete and not
#                  all PASS: deny (the cap) — the approver records ESCALATE, the person decides
#   Bash         `gh pr create`, or `git push` naming `praxis/<change>`
#                → `decision.md` at APPROVE (human): allow · APPROVE with every lens of the
#                  current round at PASS: allow · anything else: deny with the reason
#
# No active change, no ledger, or `fast` mode → these three stay silent: a project that has
# not opened a cycle has nothing to wait for, and `fast` is the trivial path, unchanged.
#
# A second gate rides on the edit event: the tier-1 comment pass (specs/comment-discipline,
# ADR-001/ADR-002). It reads the content the edit carries — `tool_input.content` for Write,
# `old_string`/`new_string` for Edit, `edits[]` for MultiEdit, the `+` lines of a Codex
# patch — and denies four patterns that need no judgment to recognise: commented-out code,
# banner and divider comments, chain-of-thought leakage, and a `TODO` with no owner and no
# tracker. Deny is proportionate only because the fix is one keystroke (ADR-002).
#
# Two rules outrank detection there. Only lines this edit ADDS are inspected, so a comment
# already in the person's file is never the reason a write is refused. And the
# false-positive gate outranks the detection gate: a Go exported doc comment, a rustdoc
# example, a regex explanation and an owned TODO are all mandated by somebody's published
# guidance (ADR-005), so every detector is anchored and loosened until they pass.
#
# Escape hatches: PRAXIS_FLOOR=off allows everything — floor, cycle gates and comment pass.
# PRAXIS_COMMENTS=off lifts the comment pass alone and leaves the rest standing.
# Those are the only two overrides — there is no phrase, flag file or prompt that lifts
# either from inside a session.
#
# Fail-open on anything unexpected (no payload, unreadable path, a host that speaks a
# different dialect): the floor is about ceremony, not safety, so a hook defect must cost
# a missing record, never a blocked editor. Each such fall-through leaves one stderr line.
#
# Hosts: Claude Code reads allow / deny / ask. Codex reads the same nested shape but its
# reference says `ask` is "parsed but not supported yet" — the hook run is marked failed and
# the tool call CONTINUES — so on Codex the approval stop is emitted as `deny` with the same
# reason, pointing at `/praxis:approve` (host detection: praxis_host in lib.sh). Codex also
# reports `tool_name: apply_patch` with the patch in `tool_input.command`; paths are read
# from its `*** … File:` headers. Codex reports its shell as `Bash` and `spawn_agent` as
# `Agent`, so the ship gate and the barrier reach it; it has no SendMessage.

set -uo pipefail

SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"
. "${SCRIPT_DIR}/lib.sh"
PRAXIS_ROOT="$(praxis_root "$SCRIPT_DIR")"

allow() { [ -n "${1:-}" ] && printf 'praxis pre-tool-use: %s — allowing\n' "$1" >&2; exit 0; }

[ "${PRAXIS_FLOOR:-}" = off ] && exit 0

payload=""
IFS= read -r -d '' -t 1 payload
[ -n "$payload" ] || allow "no payload"

tool="$(praxis_json_string "$payload" tool_name)"
case "$tool" in Edit|Write|MultiEdit|NotebookEdit|apply_patch|Agent|SendMessage|Bash) ;; *) exit 0 ;; esac

cwd="$(praxis_json_string "$payload" cwd)"
[ -n "$cwd" ] || cwd="$PWD"
root="$(praxis_project_root "${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR:-${CODEX_PROJECT_DIR:-$cwd}}")"
[ -n "$root" ] || allow "cannot resolve the project root"

mode="$(praxis_mode)"
host="$(praxis_host)"
change="$(praxis_active_change "$root")"
name="${change:-<change>}"
ledger="${root}/specs/${change}/implementation.md"
spec="$(praxis_spec_file "$root" "$change")"
target=""

# The blank forms, project-local, so the deny points at files the model may read. Copied
# once, only on a deny — an allowed edit never writes into the user's repository. The
# CLAUDE.md -> AGENTS.md symlink survives `cp -R` on both BSD and GNU; where it does not,
# a plain copy stands in.
ensure_templates() {
  [ -d "${root}/specs/_templates" ] && return 0
  local src="${PRAXIS_ROOT}/specs/_templates"
  [ -d "$src" ] || { printf 'praxis pre-tool-use: no templates at %s\n' "$src" >&2; return 0; }
  mkdir -p "${root}/specs" 2>/dev/null && cp -R "$src" "${root}/specs/_templates" 2>/dev/null \
    || { printf 'praxis pre-tool-use: could not copy templates into %s/specs\n' "$root" >&2; return 0; }
  if [ -e "${root}/specs/_templates/AGENTS.md" ] && [ ! -e "${root}/specs/_templates/CLAUDE.md" ]; then
    cp "${root}/specs/_templates/AGENTS.md" "${root}/specs/_templates/CLAUDE.md" 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
}

decide() {
  praxis_trace "PreToolUse" "$1 ${mode} ${target}"
  printf '{\n  "hookSpecificOutput": {\n    "hookEventName": "PreToolUse",\n    "permissionDecision": "%s",\n    "permissionDecisionReason": "%s"\n  }\n}\n' \
    "$1" "$(escape_for_json "$2")"
  exit 0
}
deny() { ensure_templates; decide deny "$1"; }

# Same trailer on every deny: what is free, and the only way out.
trailer="Writes under specs/, .praxis/, agents/ are always allowed. Override: PRAXIS_FLOOR=off (env, session-wide)."
override="Override: PRAXIS_FLOOR=off (env, session-wide)."

# --- The cycle's limits: barrier, round cap, ship gate ---------------------------------------
#
# Every decision below is read from files the roles write (design.md appendix); the hook adds
# only the message. A deny names the file that would open the gate and who writes it.

# A builder dispatch or resume against the ledger's round.
builder_gate() {
  local round lenses missing not_pass
  round="$(praxis_ledger_round "$root" "$change")"
  [ "$round" -gt 0 ] || exit 0                       # no review yet: the first build runs free
  lenses="$(praxis_round_lenses "$root" "$change" "$round")"
  [ -n "$lenses" ] || decide deny "PRAXIS barrier: waiting for specs/${change}/review/round-${round}/lenses — the ledger says round ${round} and no lens list exists. The orchestrator writes it (one lens name per line) before dispatching the lenses; the builder resumes only when every named lens has written specs/${change}/review/round-${round}/<lens>.md. ${override}"
  missing="$(praxis_round_missing "$root" "$change" "$round" | tr '\n' ' ')"; missing="${missing% }"
  [ -z "$missing" ] || decide deny "PRAXIS barrier: waiting for ${missing} — round ${round} of specs/${change} is incomplete. Each lens writes specs/${change}/review/round-${round}/<lens>.md (verdict: PASS | CHANGES) in its own context; the builder resumes only when every name in review/round-${round}/lenses has its file. A lens that never returns gets 'verdict: SKIP' written by the orchestrator, which counts as not PASS. ${override}"
  praxis_round_all_pass "$root" "$change" "$round" && exit 0
  [ "$round" -lt 2 ] && exit 0                       # the one fix round
  not_pass="$(praxis_round_not_pass "$root" "$change" "$round" | tr '\n' ' ')"; not_pass="${not_pass% }"
  decide deny "PRAXIS round cap: 2 rounds done → approver / ESCALATE. Round ${round} of specs/${change} is complete and not all PASS (${not_pass}). A third fix round never starts: dispatch the approver, which records ESCALATE in specs/${change}/decision.md; the person then decides with /praxis:approve ${change} or /praxis:rework ${change} \"<note>\". ${override}"
}

# A push of the change branch or a PR against the approver's decision.
ship_gate() {
  local verdict round not_pass
  verdict="$(praxis_decision_verdict "$root" "$change")"
  [ -n "$verdict" ] || decide deny "PRAXIS ship gate: specs/${change}/decision.md does not exist — nothing ships without it. The approver writes it (verdict: APPROVE | ESCALATE, with every check it re-ran) once every lens is PASS; the person can override with /praxis:approve ${change}, which records APPROVE (human). ${override}"
  case "$verdict" in
    "APPROVE (human)") exit 0 ;;
    APPROVE)
      round="$(praxis_ledger_round "$root" "$change")"
      [ "$round" -gt 0 ] || decide deny "PRAXIS ship gate: specs/${change}/decision.md records APPROVE but the ledger's round is 0 — no review round ran. APPROVE ships only with every lens of the current round at PASS; without a round the person decides with /praxis:approve ${change}. ${override}"
      praxis_round_all_pass "$root" "$change" "$round" && exit 0
      not_pass="$(praxis_round_not_pass "$root" "$change" "$round" | tr '\n' ' ')"; not_pass="${not_pass% }"
      decide deny "PRAXIS ship gate: lenses not all PASS in round ${round} of specs/${change}: ${not_pass:-no lens listed in review/round-${round}/lenses}. APPROVE ships only with every lens at PASS; a CHANGES or SKIP goes back to the builder (round < 2) or to the approver for ESCALATE. The person can override with /praxis:approve ${change}. ${override}"
      ;;
    *)
      decide deny "PRAXIS ship gate: specs/${change}/decision.md verdict ${verdict} — only APPROVE (with every lens PASS) or APPROVE (human) ships. ESCALATE means the person decides: /praxis:approve ${change} to ship, /praxis:rework ${change} \"<note>\" to send it back to the builder. ${override}"
      ;;
  esac
}

case "$tool" in
  Agent|SendMessage|Bash)
    [ "$mode" != fast ] || exit 0
    [ -n "$change" ] && [ -f "$ledger" ] || exit 0
    case "$tool" in
      Agent)
        kind="$(praxis_json_string "$payload" subagent_type | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')"; kind="${kind#praxis:}"
        [ "$kind" = builder ] || exit 0
        target="agent:${kind}"; builder_gate
        ;;
      SendMessage)
        to="$(praxis_json_string "$payload" to)"
        builder_id="$(praxis_ledger_field "$root" "$change" builder)"
        [ -n "$to" ] && [ "$to" = "$builder_id" ] || exit 0
        target="message:${to}"; builder_gate
        ;;
      Bash)
        cmd="$(praxis_json_strings "$payload" command)"
        ship=0
        case "$cmd" in *"gh pr create"*) ship=1 ;; esac
        case "$cmd" in *"git push"*) case "$cmd" in *"praxis/${change}"*) ship=1 ;; esac ;; esac
        [ "$ship" = 1 ] || exit 0
        target="bash:ship"; ship_gate
        ;;
    esac
    exit 0
    ;;
esac

paths="$(praxis_edit_paths "$payload")"
[ -n "$paths" ] || allow "no file path in tool input"

# A review file is its lens's to write. The main thread may write one only to record a lens
# that never returned, and only with `verdict: SKIP`. Claude Code puts `agent_id` on every
# hook that fires inside a subagent (hooks reference, common input fields) and nothing on the
# main thread, so its absence is the main thread. Other hosts document no such field; there
# the rule stays out rather than deny a lens its own file.
review_gate() {
  [ "$host" = claude ] || return 0
  [ -z "$(praxis_json_string "$payload" agent_id)" ] || return 0
  local rel
  while IFS= read -r rel; do
    case "$rel" in specs/*/review/round-*/*.md) ;; *) continue ;; esac
    { praxis_json_strings "$payload" content; praxis_json_strings "$payload" new_string; } \
      | awk '/^verdict:[ \t]*SKIP([ \t]|$)/ { found = 1 } END { exit !found }' && return 0
    target="$rel"
    decide deny "PRAXIS review: ${rel} is a lens's file and the main thread is writing it. Each lens writes its own review in its own context. The orchestrator writes one only to record a lens that never returned, with 'verdict: SKIP' and the reason — SKIP counts as not PASS, so the change goes to the approver, never to the builder. Dispatch the lens, or wait for it. ${override}"
  done <<EOF
$(praxis_project_targets "$root" "$cwd" "$paths")
EOF
}
review_gate

# The first source-side target decides; an all-spec-side write is free.
target="$(praxis_source_target "$root" "$cwd" "$paths")"
[ -n "$target" ] || exit 0

# The two markers are not the model's to write, in any mode.
case "$target" in
  .praxis/approved/*)
    decide deny "PRAXIS floor: ${target} is the approval record. It is written by the person — by approving the first source edit when the floor asks, or by running /praxis:approve ${name} — never by the model. Do not create or edit it."
    ;;
  .praxis/needs-human/*)
    decide deny "PRAXIS floor: ${target} is the escalation record. It is written by post-tool-use when specs/${name}/decision.md records ESCALATE, and removed when the person runs /praxis:approve ${name} or /praxis:rework ${name} — never by the model. Do not create or edit it."
    ;;
esac

# Tier-1 comment discipline. It runs before the mode case because it judges the CONTENT of
# this edit, which is true or false independently of what `specs/` holds — one call site
# instead of one at each of the floor's allow points. Everything it can fail to understand
# (a tool it does not read, a path it cannot classify, an unparseable body) returns silently
# and the floor decides on its own.
comment_gate() {
  [ "${PRAXIS_COMMENTS:-}" = off ] && return 0

  local group added found kind line label
  group="$(praxis_comment_group "$target")"
  [ "$group" = skip ] && return 0

  added="$(praxis_added_lines "$payload" "$tool")"
  [ -n "$added" ] || return 0
  case "$added" in *"@generated"*) return 0 ;; esac

  found="$(printf '%s\n' "$added" | praxis_scan_comments "$group")"
  [ -n "$found" ] || return 0
  kind="${found%%$'\t'*}"
  line="${found#*$'\t'}"

  # The craft's own wording, verbatim: a second vocabulary for the same four patterns
  # would be a second standard. crafts/comment-discipline/comment-discipline.md.
  case "$kind" in
    code)   label="Commented-out code — git holds the history." ;;
    banner) label="Banner and divider comments — \`// =====\`, \`/* ---- */\`." ;;
    cot)    label="Chain-of-thought leakage — \`// Step 1:\`, \`// Now let's\`, \`// As requested\`, and anything else referring to the conversation instead of to the code." ;;
    todo)   label="A \`TODO\` with no owner and no tracker. What earns one carries either: \`// TODO(username): Describe what needs to be done.\` (Google C++) or \`# TODO: crbug.com/192795 - Investigate cpufreq optimizations.\` (Google Python)." ;;
    *)      return 0 ;;
  esac

  decide deny "PRAXIS comment discipline: this edit adds a line to ${target} that carries a tier-1 pattern.

    ${line}

${label}
The fix in every case is to delete the line. Re-send the edit without it; every other line in this edit is fine.
Only lines this edit adds are read — a comment already in the file is never the reason.
Override: PRAXIS_COMMENTS=off lifts this check alone; PRAXIS_FLOOR=off lifts the whole floor (env, session-wide)."
}
comment_gate

# The spec's shape, repeated in the deny for a model that cannot read the template. The
# template itself ships with factory-cycle (T05); until the plugin carries spec.md the deny
# points at design.md as the form to read, and still names spec.md as the file to write.
sections="Goal, Scope (In / Out), Acceptance (runnable checks: command → expected output; the approver executes these), Approach (files and the seam, no code), Open questions ([NEEDS CLARIFICATION] items; status: ready when none remain)"
spec_template="specs/_templates/spec.md"
[ -f "${PRAXIS_ROOT}/specs/_templates/spec.md" ] || [ -f "${root}/specs/_templates/spec.md" ] || spec_template="specs/_templates/design.md"

case "$mode" in
  fast)
    [ -n "$change" ] && [ -f "$ledger" ] && exit 0
    deny "PRAXIS floor (fast): ${target} is a source edit and specs/${name}/implementation.md does not exist.
1. Read specs/_templates/implementation.md, then Write specs/${name}/implementation.md in that shape (kebab-case change name): Status, one task line for this edit, Completed + verify evidence after the verify command runs.
2. Write .praxis/active-change containing the single line: ${name}
${trailer}"
    ;;
  *)
    if [ -n "$change" ] && [ -n "$spec" ] && [ -f "$ledger" ]; then
      praxis_approved "$root" "$change" && exit 0
      reason="${spec} is written and awaits the person's approval. Approving this edit approves the spec: the approval writes .praxis/approved/${change} and the floor will not ask again. On a host without a prompt, or in an unattended run, the person runs /praxis:approve ${change}. After approval, set 'stage: planned' in specs/${change}/implementation.md as the record — the stage unlocks nothing; only the approval does."
      case "$host" in
        claude) decide ask "$reason" ;;
        *) decide deny "${reason} (This host cannot ask, so the floor denies: stop and wait for the person to run /praxis:approve ${change}.)" ;;
      esac
    fi
    if [ -z "$change" ] || [ ! -f "$ledger" ]; then
      deny "PRAXIS floor (${mode}): ${target} is a source edit and specs/${name}/ has no spec yet.
1. Read ${spec_template}, then Write specs/${name}/spec.md (kebab-case change name) with: ${sections}. A legacy specs/${name}/design.md is accepted in its place.
2. Read specs/_templates/implementation.md, then Write specs/${name}/implementation.md with 'stage: designed' in its frontmatter and the task list.
3. Write .praxis/active-change containing the single line: ${name}
4. STOP: the first source edit after that asks the person to approve the spec.
${trailer}"
    else
      deny "PRAXIS floor (${mode}): ${target} is a source edit and specs/${change}/spec.md does not exist (nor the legacy design.md).
1. Read ${spec_template}, then Write specs/${change}/spec.md with: ${sections}.
2. Set 'stage: designed' in the frontmatter of specs/${change}/implementation.md.
3. STOP: the first source edit after that asks the person to approve the spec.
${trailer}"
    fi
    ;;
esac
exit 0
